\section{Time Does Not Advance for Every Update}

An event-driven simulator advances the simulation clock to the next scheduled time. At that time, it can perform several rounds of work before time advances again: a signal update makes a process eligible to run, and that process can schedule another zero-delay update. In VHDL these same-time simulation cycles are called delta cycles. For example, with concurrent assignments \verb|b <= a; c <= b;| the change of \texttt{a} leads to a later delta update of \texttt{b}, which leads to a delta update of \texttt{c}, all at the same modeled physical time \cite{ieee10762008}. Delta cycles are not clock cycles and do not measure gate delay.

SystemVerilog uses a time slot divided into scheduling regions rather than the VHDL simulation-cycle model. In ordinary module code, clocked processes run in the Active region; executing a nonblocking assignment in that region evaluates the right-hand side and schedules the variable update for the NBA region. A change from an NBA can in turn cause further same-time activity. The ordered regions matter even if a waveform is drawn with all the same-time changes at the same horizontal coordinate \cite{ieee1800systemverilog}.

\section{A Two-Register Experiment}

Robert G. Plantz's \emph{Introduction to Computer Organization} discusses clocked storage in chapter~7. The hardware intuition is that two flip-flops on a common edge sample their respective inputs; the second does not intentionally borrow the newly captured value of the first on that edge. Meeting actual setup and hold constraints is a separate physical requirement. Here is the SystemVerilog RTL model, which is simulatable and normally synthesizable:

\begin{verbatim}
module shift2 (
    input  logic clk, d,
    output logic first, second
);
    always_ff @(posedge clk) begin
        first  <= d;
        second <= first;
    end
endmodule
\end{verbatim}

This companion is simulation only. Its delays schedule deliberate stimulus, not circuitry, and the displayed time is not used as a test oracle.

\begin{verbatim}
module shift2_tb;
    timeunit 1ns;
    timeprecision 1ps;
    logic clk, d;
    wire first, second;
    shift2 dut (.clk(clk), .d(d), .first(first), .second(second));
    initial begin
        clk = 0; d = 0;
        #2 d = 1;
        #3 clk = 1;
        #2 clk = 0;
        #1 d = 0;
        #2 clk = 1;
        #1 $finish;
    end
    always @(posedge clk) begin
        $display("active: %b %b", first, second);
        $strobe("settled: %b %b", first, second);
    end
endmodule
\end{verbatim}

Although the DUT and monitor may execute in either Active-region order, neither assignment updates its destination in that region. Thus \verb|$display| reads the pre-update state, while \verb|$strobe| observes the values in Postponed, at the end of the time slot. This argument relies on driving \texttt{d} before, not in competition with, the rising edge. The two observations are simulator outputs for one modelled edge; they do not imply two hardware states or a physical delay between the registers.

